 normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186262674 :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186262675 :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"] [17592186262676 :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186262682 :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+4t \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}-2t \\\\ 3+t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ -2t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186262683 :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5t \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186262684 :question/worked-solution "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3+7t \\\\ -5t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7t \\\\ -5t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186262694 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ 5-4t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}t \\\\ -4t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,5,\\frac{1}{7})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262695 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+t \\\\ -5+3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262696 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 5-4t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=5-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262703 :question/worked-solution "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+t \\\\ 3-5t \\\\ 5+6t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=5+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262704 :question/worked-solution "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-3t \\\\ -11+2t \\\\ 3+7t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3t \\\\ y=-11+2t \\\\ z=3+7t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262705 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(4,7,-6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-1,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262707 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{7}=\\frac{y-(-8)}{-4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{11},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-8,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,-4,11⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=10+7t \\\\ y=-8-4t \\\\ z=-1+11t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262743 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [17592186262743 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12+4-12 \\\\ -4-5+4 \\\\ -2+4+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262746 :question/problem "Find $Mz$ if \n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$z=\\begin{bmatrix}s \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262746 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Mz & =\\begin{bmatrix}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}s \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}s\\cdot s+t\\cdot t \\\\ (-t)\\cdot s+s\\cdot t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ -st+st\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262747 :question/problem "Find $Qg$ if \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$g=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262747 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Qg & =\\begin{bmatrix}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}m\\cdot (-2)+2m\\cdot 3 \\\\ -m\\cdot (-2)+n\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2m+6m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262749 :question/problem "If $S=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $T=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ then $ST=$"] [17592186262749 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-6) & (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-6) & 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+12 & -4+8 \\\\ 12+(-6) & 12+(-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."] [17592186262750 :question/problem "If $L=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$\nand\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$\nthen $LM=$"] [17592186262750 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-36)+7\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-36)+3\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18+21 & 2+7 \\\\ -9+9 & 1+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."] [17592186262766 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line."] [17592186262766 :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(-2,3,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,-5,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186262767 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-2t \\\\ 5-t \\\\ -7+t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,5,-7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-1,1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}.\n$$"] [17592186262768 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262769 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line."] [17592186262769 :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(7,-2,4)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186262770 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y-0}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}.\n$$"] [17592186262771 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [17592186262771 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+3t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1-2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$"] [17592186262774 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [17592186262774 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=5:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+7t \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5\n$$"] [17592186262775 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [17592186262775 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=-2:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}3-5t \\\\ t \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ part and instead write $z=-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2\n$$"] [17592186262800 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n    $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262802 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n    $D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262803 :question/problem "If\n    $U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$"] [17592186262804 :question/problem "If\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(T)=-4$, what is the value of $v?$"] [17592186262805 :question/problem "If\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(P)=-9$, what is the value of $r?$"] [17592186262806 :question/problem "If\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}5-p & p \\\\ 3 & p\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(B)=1$, what is the value of $p?$"] [17592186262807 :question/problem "If\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3y & y \\\\ 3 & y-5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(A)=-27$, what is the value of $y?$"] [17592186262812 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${d}_{23}$ in the matrix\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -12 & 44 & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & 41\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262812 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ (second row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-33$ are removed: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 30-2\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =300-50 \\\\  & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262813 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 72\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262813 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ (third row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $8$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 7 & -9 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 9-(-9)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =63-18 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262820 :question/problem "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262820 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262821 :question/problem "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262821 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 3=-3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262822 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262823 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262824 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262825 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262826 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262839 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}k-1 & k-1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k.$"] [17592186262840 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 27 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$"] [17592186262841 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & a \\\\ a & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $a.$"] [17592186262842 :question/problem "If $M=\\begin{bmatrix}t & 5 \\\\ t & 9\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$"] [17592186262843 :question/problem "If $P=\\begin{bmatrix}n & -4 \\\\ -16 & n\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$"] [17592186262844 :question/problem "If $M=\\begin{bmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ t & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $t.$"] [17592186262850 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186262851 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186262852 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186262854 :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point."] [17592186262854 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186262856 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist"] [17592186262856 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186262866 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${a}_{12}$ in the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3d & 5b \\\\ b & {c}^{2} & b-d \\\\ b & -10c & b+d\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186262866 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3d & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗ & b-d \\\\ b & ∗ & b+d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\cdot (b+d)-(b-d)\\cdot b \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+bd-{b}^{2}+db \\\\  & =2bd\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262867 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${e}_{13}$ in the matrix\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2 & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & 3 \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & -10y\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186262867 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and the third column:\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & ∗ \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(w-1)\\cdot (w-1)-(1-w)\\cdot (1-w) \\\\  & =(w-1)^{2}-(1-w)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262868 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${t}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}3p & r & 3p \\\\ q & r & -q \\\\ -6q & -p & 6q\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186262868 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and the second column:\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3p & ∗ & 3p \\\\ ∗ & r & ∗ \\\\ -6q & ∗ & 6q\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3p\\cdot 6q-3p\\cdot (-6q) \\\\  & =18pq+18pq \\\\  & =36pq\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262869 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${m}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-50 & g & f \\\\ f & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & g & -5\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186262869 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & g & f \\\\ ∗ & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =g\\cdot {f}^{2}-f\\cdot {g}^{2} \\\\  & =g{f}^{2}-f{g}^{2} \\\\  & =gf(f-g)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262870 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5t & 18 & 6t \\\\ -3 & 9 & 5 \\\\ 19t & 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186262870 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and first column:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 9 & 5 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{11}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3t-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27t-5 & =4 \\\\ 27t & =9 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186262871 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, find all the possible values of $p$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}{p}^{2} & {p}^{3} & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2+p & p-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186262871 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & {p}^{3} & ∗ \\\\ -6 & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 5 & ∗ & p-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${d}_{12}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot (p-1)-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6p-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6p-19 & =-4 \\\\ -6p & =15 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [17592186262872 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, find all the possible values of $y$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}y & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & 4-y & 12y \\\\ y & 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186262872 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${m}_{21}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-6y)-y\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-11y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11y & =11 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-1.$"] [17592186262875 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, find all the possible values of $z$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & z-8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 6z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186262875 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and third column:\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 & ∗ \\\\ -5 & z-8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 6z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${r}_{33}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\cdot (z-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-8z+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-8z+15 & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}-8z+12 & =0 \\\\ (z-2)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=2$ and $z=6.$"] [17592186262876 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$"] [17592186262876 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true."] [17592186262877 :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **III.** The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points"] [17592186262877 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true."] [17592186262879 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n- **I.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}$ does not exist\n- **II.** The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262879 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$"] [17592186262880 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$"] [17592186262881 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186262900 :question/problem "Find the value of $(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}$ at $t=1,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3+t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t-3 \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ (t+1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262900 :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}=2{f}^{′}(t)+5{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2f(1)+5g(1))^{′} & =2{f}^{′}(1)+5{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262901 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262904 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $S(1,-3,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186262904 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186262905 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186262905 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262906 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(Q)=-5$\n- **II.** $det(Q)=1$\n- **III.** $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262908 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=-30$\n\n \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262913 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(R)=0$\n- **III.** $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186262915 :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩$ and $w=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9i-j-23k \\\\  & =⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.$"] [17592186262916 :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8i-2j-22k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$"] [17592186262917 :question/problem "Given that\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$"] [17592186262918 :question/problem "Given that \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$"] [17592186262924 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$"] [17592186262924 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262936 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(1,2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0.$"] [17592186262936 :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-2,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+8|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262937 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatri