and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(7,-2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{-2+1}{7-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-2) \\\\ 5y+5 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+5y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295181 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-81.$"] [17592186295181 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\en