nd{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 18 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}{R}^{′}$ are ${P}^{′}(2,2),$\n${Q}^{′}(18,8),$ ${R}^{′}(-8,-4).$"] [17592186295205 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $GHI$ has vertices at $G(5,5),\\,H(-1,-4),$ and $I(3,5).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding vertices of  $△{G}^{′}{H}^{′}{I}^{′}$, the image of $△GHI$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${G}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$),\\,$  $\\,{H}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}}\n$),\\,$ $\\,{I}^{′}($ \n{{field-5}}, \n{{field-6}}\n$)$"] [17592186295205 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $△GHI$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $GHI:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 15 & -17 \\\\ 25 & -14 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{G}^{′}{H}^{′}{I}^{′}$ are ${G}^{′}(-15,25),$\n${H}^{′}(15,-14),$ ${I}^{′}(-17,21).$"] [17592186295206 :question/problem "Consider the line $x=4$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=16.$"] [17592186295206 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $x=4$ passes through the points $G(4,0)$ and $H(4,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{′}(12,4)$ and ${H}^{′}(10,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${G}^{′}$ and ${H}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{6-4}{10-12}(x-12) \\\\ y-4 & =-1(x-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-x+12 \\\\ x+y & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295207 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-8.$"] [17592186295207 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=-1$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(0,2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,3).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3-2}{4-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-8 & =x \\\\ x-4y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295208 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=21.$"] [17592186295208 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=3$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,3)$ and ${Q}^{′}(10,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1-3}{10-9}(x-9) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-2x+18 \\\\ 2x+y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295209 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=14.$"] [17592186295209 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=2$ passes through the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(2,4)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1-4}{4-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-3x+6 \\\\ 3x+2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295210 :question/problem "Consider the line $3x+2y-6=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=42.$"] [17592186295210 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $3x+2y-6=0$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(2,0)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,-6)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{2-(-6)}{4-9}(x-9) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{8}{-5}(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =-\\frac{8}{5}(x-9) \\\\ 5y+30 & =-8x+72 \\\\ 8x+5y & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x+5y=42\n$$"] [17592186295211 :question/problem "Consider the line $-4x+y+1=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=6.$"] [17592186295211 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $-4x+y+1=0$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(-1,-2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(6,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{6-(-1)}(x+1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{8}{7}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y+14 & =8x+8 \\\\ 8x-7y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x-7y=6\n$$"] [17592186295213 :question/problem "Given that $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $n.$\n\n|  |  | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $u=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |"] [17592186295213 :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,n\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $n$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}n \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295247 :question/problem "Let $T$ be the linear transformation with matrix representation $T$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{KL}$ has endpoints at $K(1,0)$ and $L(0,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints $\\overset{―}{{K}^{′}{L}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{KL}$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${K}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,$ and $\\,{L}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}}\n$)$"] [17592186295247 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{KL}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 & 2\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & 3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${K}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${L}^{′}(-2,2).$"] [17592186295249 :question/problem "If\n    $C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$,\nthen find the transpose of $C.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${C}^{T}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186295249 :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186295250 :question/problem "If\n    $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$,\nthen find the transpose of $E.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${E}^{T}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-