atrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299569 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-6x \\\\ 5y+9x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299569 :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-6x \\\\ 9x+5y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6x \\\\ 9x+5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6x \\\\ 9x+5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299591 :question/problem "Calculate $det(R)$ given that\n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(R)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299592 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299593 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299596 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299597 :question/problem "Calculate $det(E)$ given that\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(E)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299598 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3x & 2y \\\\ -5 & x\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299600 :question/problem "If\n    $D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & m \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(D)=-11,$ determine the value of $m.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299601 :question/problem "If\n$U=\\begin{bmatrix}1+r & 2-r \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(U)=-6$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299602 :question/problem "If\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}3+s & 2s \\\\ -4 & 3-s\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(S)=25,$ what is the value of $s?$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299603 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299603 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{12+10}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{19}{22}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299604 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299604 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+15}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ \\frac{25}{17}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299605 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299605 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(7)-(3)(5)}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299606 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299606 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12+2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{19}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299607 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299607 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the inverse of $T$:\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(5)(1)-(6)(2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299608 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=10 \\\\ 5x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299609 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. \nThe variable $x$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-6y=4 \\\\ 3x+5y\\,=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299610 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ Similarly, the variable $x$ does not appear in the second equation, which means that its coefficient is $0$ too. \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x+0y=10 \\\\ 0x+4y=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299611 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299611 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(-1)-(1)(1)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299612 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299612 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299613 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299613 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(1)(-3)-(2)(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3+2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299614 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation.\n\nThe variable $x$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ Similarly, the variable $y$ does not appear in the second equation, which means that its coefficient is $0$ too. So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+8y=-8 \\\\ -4x+0y=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299615 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-5 \\\\ 3x-2y=7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299616 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299616 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(1)-(1)(-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299617 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=9 \\\\ 0x-5y=-15\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299618 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y=3 \\\\ 2x+9y=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299619 :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+0y=4 \\\\ 0x+y=-10\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299621 :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system of equations is\n\n| { | {{field-1}} | $=$ | $\\,4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $=$ | $\\,\\,3.$ |  |"] [17592186299622 :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system of equations is\n\n| { | {{field-1}} | $=$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $=$ | $\\,\\,-7.$ |  |"] [17592186299623 :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system of equations is\n\n| { | {{field-1}} | $=$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $=$ | $\\,\\,-7.$ |  |"] [17592186299624 :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system of equations is\n\n| { | {{field-1}} | $=$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $=$ | $\\,\\,-7.$ |  |"] [17592186299626 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299626 :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $3$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $3$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299627 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299627 :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the $x$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299628 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |"] [17592186299628 :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the $y$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\sqrt{3}$ in the $y$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299629 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -2x+0y+6z=9 \\\\ 0x+7y-5z=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299630 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+4z=10 \\\\ 2x-5y+0z=-5 \\\\ -4x+3y+7z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299633 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$"] [17592186299634 :question/problem "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{s}\\,r=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$"] [17592186299637 :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).$\n\n| $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299637 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=3T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299638 :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}).$\n\n| $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299638 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=-3\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(-3\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-3T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299639 :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}).$\n\n| $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299639 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})=2T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299640 :question/problem "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}).$\n\n| $\\,T(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299640 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299641 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299641 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}) & =-5T({v}_{1})+4T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} & =-5T({v}_{1})+4\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299642 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299642 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =-6T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} & =-6T({v}_{1})+5\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =5\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299643 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299643 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =3T({v}_{1})+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299644 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299644 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} & =3T({v}_{1})+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299645 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find  $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299645 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} & =4{v}_{1}+5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =4(0)+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=T(5{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299646 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find  $T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299646 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =12{v}_{1}-5{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})-5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =12(0)-5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =-5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=T(-5{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{1}) & =-5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -20 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -20 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299647 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find  $T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299647 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2} & =15{v}_{1}-6{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2} \\\\  & =15({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})-6{v}_{1} \\\\  & =15(0)-6{v}_{1} \\\\  & =-6{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=T(-6{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}) & =-6T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =-6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299648 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find $\\,T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299648 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2} & =13{v}_{1}-7{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2} \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-7({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-7(0) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=T(13{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(13{v}_{1}) & =13T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =13\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(6{v}_{1}-7{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299649 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find $\\,T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299649 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2} & =13{v}_{1}-4{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-4(0) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=T(13{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(13{v}_{1}) & =13T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =13\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 65 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 65 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299650 :question/problem "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $9$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-4}} | $14$ |  |  |\n|  | $5$ | {{field-5}} |  |  |"] [17592186299650 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 2}={AB}_{4\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 9 \\\\ ∗ & 14 \\\\ 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ ∗ & 9 \\\\ ∗ & 14 \\\\ 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ ∗ & 14 \\\\ 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,0=0\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=(-1)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=5\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299651 :question/problem "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $9$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299651 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -8\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-8=0\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299652 :question/problem "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $4$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299652 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,5=2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 5 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,7=4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299653 :question/problem "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-6$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $11$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299653 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-2=4\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299659 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $u$ onto $v.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,u=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$"] [17592186299660 :question/problem "If $c=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ $d=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and $c⊥d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [17592186299675 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186299675 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3 & -3\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3 & 0\\cdot (-5)+4\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3+6 & 15+4 \\\\ 0+12 & 0+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 19 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299676 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186299676 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 & 3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 5 & (-1)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9+(-10) & 6+2 \\\\ 3+20 & -2+(-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & 8 \\\\ 23 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299677 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n| $CD=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186299677 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-3)+(-\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot 1\\, & \\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+(-\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1\\, & \\,2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\, & \\,\\frac{5}{4}-3 \\\\ -6+\\frac{1}{3}\\, & \\,5+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{4} & -\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{3} & \\frac{19}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299678 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n| $CD=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186299678 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 15 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 3\\, & \\,\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 15+2\\cdot 6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 10+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 3\\, & \\,(-1)\\cdot 15+\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6+6\\, & \\,9+12 \\\\ -10+4\\, & \\,-15+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 21 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299679 :question/problem "Let $F=GH.$ What is the value of ${f}_{21}$, if\n\n$$\nG=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,H=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n${f}_{21}=$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299679 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $2$nd row in $G$ by the $1$st column in $H$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{21} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299680 :question/problem "Let $G=HM.$ What is the value of ${g}_{31}$, if\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n${g}_{31}=$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299680 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $H$ by the $1$st column in $M$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{31} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3+15+0+0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299681 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+0+0 & 3+0+0 & 6+0+0 \\\\ 0+6+4 & -1-4+0 & -2+0+12 \\\\ 0+15-2 & 0-10+0 & 0+0-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 13 & -10 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299682 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+3+4 & 6-1+2 & 3+2+0 \\\\ 4-3+10 & 8+1+5 & 4-2+0 \\\\ 2+0+6 & 4+0+3 & 2+0+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 14 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299683 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+0+2 & 4+0+1 & 2+0+3 \\\\ 3-3+8 & 6+0+4 & 3-2+12 \\\\ 0+6+10 & 0+0+5 & 0+4+15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 10 & 13 \\\\ 16 & 5 & 19\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299685 :question/problem "Let $P=QR.$ What is the value of ${p}_{31}$, if\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n${p}_{31}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299685 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $Q$ by the $1$st column in $R$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{31} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(5)+1(-3)+(-2)(1) \\\\  & =10-3-2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299686 :question/problem "Let $X=YZ.$ What is the value of ${x}_{12}$, if\n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n${x}_{12}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299686 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $1$st row in $Y$ by the $2$nd column in $Z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)(5)+3(-3)+0(1) \\\\  & =-10-9+0 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299687 :question/problem "Let $Z=XY.$ What is the value of ${z}_{24}$, if\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n${z}_{24}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299687 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $2$nd row in $X$ by the $4$th column in $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{24} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-3)+2(2) \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299688 :question/problem "If\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind $AB.$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299688 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,5=5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -15 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-15=5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -15 \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-2=-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -15 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,6=-2\\cdot (-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -15 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186299689 :question/problem "Find $PQ,$ if\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $PQ=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186299689 :question/worked-solution "Here, $P$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix and $Q$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix.  \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $P$ and $1$ row in matrix $Q$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{P}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Q}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={PQ}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot (-4) & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-4) & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4) & (-1)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299690 :question/problem "If \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}b & a & -b \\\\ -b & a & b \\\\ -b & 0 & -b\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind $Ax.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Ax=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299690 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}b & a & -b \\\\ -b & a & b \\\\ -b & 0 & -b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (-b) & + & a\\cdot 0 & + & (-b)\\cdot b \\\\ (-b)\\cdot (-b) & + & a\\cdot 0 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,b\\cdot b \\\\ (-b)\\cdot (-b) & + & 0\\cdot 0 & + & (-b)\\cdot b\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}-{b}^{2} & + & 0 & + & (-{b}^{2}) \\\\ {b}^{2} & + & 0 & + & {b}^{2} \\\\ {b}^{2} & + & 0 & + & (-{b}^{2})\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{b}^{2} \\\\ 2{b}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299691 :question/problem "If \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}m & n & -m \\\\ m & -n & -m \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ p \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind $Pu.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Pu=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299691 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pu & =\\begin{bmatrix}m & n & -m \\\\ m & -n & -m \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ p \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}m\\cdot 1 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,n\\cdot p & + & (-m)\\cdot 1 \\\\ m\\cdot 1 & + & (-n)\\cdot p & + & (-m)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 1 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\cdot p & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}m & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,np & + & (-m) \\\\ m & + & (-np) & + & (-m) \\\\ 0 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2p & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}np \\\\ -np \\\\ 2p\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299692 :question/problem "Find $By$  if \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}6p & -4q \\\\ -5p & 2q\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}p \\\\ q\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $By=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299692 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}By & =\\begin{bmatrix}6p & -4q \\\\ -5p & 2q\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}p \\\\ q\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}6p\\cdot p & + & (-4q)\\cdot q \\\\ -5p\\cdot p & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2q\\cdot q\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{p}^{2}-4{q}^{2} \\\\ -5{p}^{2}+2{q}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299694 :question/problem "Find $Nz$ if\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$z=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Nz=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299694 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Nz & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\cdot (-4) & + & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & + & (-6)\\cdot (-4) & + & 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ 4\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\cdot (-4) & + & 7\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}-24 & + & (-4) & + & 4 \\\\ 9 & + & \\,\\,\\,24 & + & 10 \\\\ 12 & + & (-8) & + & 14\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 43 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299695 :question/problem "Compute the following:\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [17592186299695 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6\\cdot (-1) & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\cdot 4 \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 & + & (-3)\\cdot (-1) & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8\\cdot (-1) & + & (-2)\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & + & (-6) & + & 8 \\\\ 15 & + & 3 & + & 28 \\\\ 3 & + & (-8) & + & (-8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 46 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299698 :question/problem "Compute the following:\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299698 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 5+11\\cdot (-1) \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299699 :question/problem "Compute the following:\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186299699 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-7)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ 5\\cdot 4+8\\cdot (-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-34 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299700 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299700 :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299715 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186299716 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{33}$ of the entry ${b}_{33}.$\n\n${M}_{33}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299716 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${b}_{33}=6$ (third row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $6$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & -1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{33}=6$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{33} & =\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2-5 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299717 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nfind the minor ${M}_{23}$ of the entry ${a}_{23}.$\n\n${M}_{23}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299717 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${a}_{23}=-1$ (second row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-1$ are removed: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${a}_{23}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{23} & =\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =12-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299718 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nfind the minor ${M}_{22}$ of the entry ${b}_{22}.$\n\n${M}_{22}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299718 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${b}_{22}=0$ (second row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $0$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ & -3 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 7 & ∗ & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{22}=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{22} & =\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =6-(-21) \\\\  & =6+21 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299719 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nfind the minor ${M}_{32}$ of the entry ${y}_{32}.$\n\n${M}_{32}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299719 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${y}_{32}=-1$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-1$ are removed: \n\n$$\nY=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 3 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ ∗ & -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${y}_{32}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{32} & =\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =0-15 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299720 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nfind the minor ${M}_{12}$ of the entry ${c}_{12}.$\n\n${M}_{12}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299720 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${c}_{12}=2$ (first row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $2$ are removed: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 7 & ∗ & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${c}_{12}=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{12} & =\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =0-28 \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299721 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & v & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{13}$ of the element ${f}_{13}.$\n\n${M}_{13}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299721 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${f}_{13},$ we remove the first row and third column: \n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & -2 \\\\ 3 & v & ∗ \\\\ 5 & 6 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${f}_{13}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{13} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & v \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & v \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-v\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-5v\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299722 :question/problem "$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}y & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the minor of ${f}_{32}$ is $10$, find the value of $y$ in the matrix above.\n\n$y=$\n {{field-1}}"] [17592186299722 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${f}_{32},$ we remove the third row and second column: \n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}y & ∗ & 1 \\\\ 2 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${f}_{32}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}y & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${f}_{32}$ is $10$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-2 & =10 \\\\ 4y & =12 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=3.$"] [17592186299723 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the minor of ${a}_{32}$ is $10$, find the value of $x$ in the matrix above.\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}"] [17592186299723 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${a}_{32},$ we remove the third row and second column: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}x & ∗ & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${a}_{32}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & =x\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{32}$ is $10$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & =10 \\\\ 6x & =22 \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{11}{3}.$"] [17592186299724 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & r & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the minor of ${b}_{23}$ is $15$, find the value of $r$ in the matrix above.\n\n$r=$\n {{field-1}}"] [17592186299724 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${b}_{23},$ we remove the second row and third column: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & r & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & r \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =1\\cdot 8-r\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =8-7r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${b}_{23}$ is $15$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-7r & =15 \\\\ -7r & =7 \\\\ r & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $r=-1.$"] [17592186299725 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${a}_{11}$ is $0$, find all the possible values of $p$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & p & 7 \\\\ 6 & -8 & p-15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values of $p$ are\n\n$p=$ \n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,p=$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186299725 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${a}_{11},$ we remove the first row and first column:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & p & 7 \\\\ ∗ & -8 & p-15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${a}_{11}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}p & 7 \\\\ -8 & p-15\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}p & 7 \\\\ -8 & p-15\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =p(p-15)-7(-8) \\\\  & ={p}^{2}-15p+56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{11}$ is $0$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-15p+56 & =0 \\\\ (p-7)(p-8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $p=7$ and $p=8.$"] [17592186299727 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4p & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 2q\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{23}$ of the element ${b}_{23}.$\n\n${M}_{23}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299727 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${b}_{23},$ we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{23} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2p & q \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2p)\\cdot 7-q\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =14p-6q.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299728 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x & 3 \\\\ y & 5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2y & x\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{23}$ of the element ${p}_{23}.$\n\n${M}_{23}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299728 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${p}_{23},$ we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2y & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{23} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2y)-x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299733 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & e & f \\\\ 5 & 3d & 2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & d\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{31}$ of the element ${t}_{31}.$\n\n${M}_{31}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299733 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${t}_{31},$ we remove the third row and first column:\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & e & f \\\\ ∗ & 3d & 2 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${t}_{31}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{31} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}e & f \\\\ 3d & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ 3d & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =e\\cdot 2-f\\cdot (3d) \\\\  & =2e-3df.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299734 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}j & 1 & 3i \\\\ 2 & k & 5 \\\\ 4 & j & k\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{21}$ of the element ${v}_{21}.$\n\n${M}_{21}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299734 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${v}_{21},$ we remove the second row and first column:\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 & 3i \\\\ 2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & j & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${v}_{21}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{21} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3i \\\\ j & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3i \\\\ j & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(3i)\\cdot j \\\\  & =k-3ij.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299735 :question/problem "Given that $c=2i-5j+k$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $4c-3d.$\n\n$4c-3d=⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$⟩$"] [17592186299735 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\nc=2i-5j+k=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $4c-3d,$ we need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c-3d & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -23 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-23,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299739 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299740 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ x \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299747 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-5.$\n\n|  |  | $-17$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $12$ | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |\n|  |  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |"] [17592186299747 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 12 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299749 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $7$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |"] [17592186299749 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299750 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=7.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\,$ |  | $-11$ | {{field-2}} | $3$ |  |\n|  |  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |"] [17592186299750 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -11 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299753 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ {{field-2}} |  | $-1$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |"] [17592186299753 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299754 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299756 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n${a}_{12}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299757 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299758 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299761 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $det(A)=1-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299762 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299764 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299765 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299766 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299766 :question/worked-solution "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299776 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $17.$\n\n${a}_{13}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299777 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299778 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299781 :question/problem "Calculate $F+G,$ where $F=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $F+G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299781 :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}F+G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+(-9) & -4+10 \\\\ -2+5 & 7+(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299782 :question/problem "Calculate $P+Q,$ where\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $P+Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [17592186299782 :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+10 & 9+(-5) & 3+(-8) \\\\ 7+(-1) & -2+4 & 11+(-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299783 :question/problem "Calculate $C-D$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $C-D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [17592186299783 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-D & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-(-9) & 12-5 & -1-2 \\\\ 6-(-8) & -7-(-1) & 3-10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299784 :question/problem "Calculate $G-H$, where\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $H=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $G-H=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [17592186299784 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-H & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-10 & 7-(-5) \\\\ -6-(-6) & 2-(-9) \\\\ 5-3 & -8-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299785 :question/problem "Calculate $P-Q$, where\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $P-Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $11$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | $5$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [17592186299785 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6-1 & 4-(-8) & 9-6 \\\\ 7-(-4) & -2-3 & 5-(-7) \\\\ 10-12 & 0-(-5) & -3-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & \\,\\,12 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,11 & -5 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299786 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186299786 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."] [17592186299801 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299802 :question/problem "Evaluate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299802 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299803 :question/problem "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |"] [17592186299803 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299807 :question/problem "Calculate $CB-CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $CB-CA=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |"] [17592186299807 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nCB-CA=C(B-A).\n$$\n\nSo, compute $B-A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB-CA & =C(B-A) \\\\  & =C(-3{I}_{3}) \\\\  & =-3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299808 :question/problem "Calculate $AC-BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $4$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC-BC=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $-4$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $4$ |  |"] [17592186299808 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law on the right:\n\n$$\nAC-BC=(A-B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, compute $A-B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC-BC & =(A-B)C \\\\  & =(4{I}_{3})C \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299809 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299810 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299810 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299811 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186299811 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ -52 \\\\ -182\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299812 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186299814 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186299814 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 48 & -60\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299815 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [17592186299815 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-58 & 136\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299816 :question/problem "Calculate $3A$, where\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $3A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299816 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299819 :question/problem "Calculate $-7A$, where\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $-7A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299819 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7A & =-7\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-7)\\cdot 5 & (-7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot (-6) & (-7)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-35 & -28 \\\\ 42 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299820 :question/problem "Calculate $2C$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $2C=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299820 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $C$ by $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2C & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299831 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${B}^{-1}$ by performing row operations."] [17592186299831 :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{B}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299833 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299834 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations."] [17592186299834 :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186299837 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299839 :question/problem "Let\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThen, the dimensions of $CD$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186299840 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186299841 :question/problem "Let\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThen, the dimensions of $FE$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186299842 :question/problem "Let  $E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [17592186299842 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the first row of the identity matrix  by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:=3{R}_{1}$ to $A.$"] [17592186299843 :question/problem "Let\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, the dimensions of $EF$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186299844 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9-2 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-11|=11\n$$"] [17592186299845 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨4,-1⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨-2,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-14|=14\n$$"] [17592186299846 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186299846 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-15 \\\\  & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-23\\,|=23\n$$"] [17592186299847 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186299847 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,13\\,|=13\n$$"] [17592186299848 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186299848 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-33\\,|=33\n$$"] [17592186299849 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-(-21) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"] [17592186299850 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8-(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-2\\,|=2\n$$"] [17592186299851 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-20-6 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-26\\,|=26\n$$"] [17592186299853 :question/problem "If $kB=\\begin{bmatrix}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $B=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299853 :question/worked-solution "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11k & 12k \\\\ 4k & -8k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11k & 12k \\\\ -4k & -8k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the bottom-left elements, $-4k$ and $-12.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k & =-12 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [17592186299854 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299855 :question/problem "If $kW=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299855 :question/worked-solution "To express $kW,$ we multiply each entry of $W$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kW & =k\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kW$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the  leftmost elements of the third row, $2k$ and $10.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =10 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [17592186299856 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299857 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 10-a\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ a\\sqrt{5} & =-30\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ a & =-6\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299858 :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299858 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 6-a\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{2}-3a & =0 \\\\ -3a & =-12\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299859 :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299859 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{7})(-10)-a\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{7}-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{7}-5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =10\\sqrt{7} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299860 :question/problem "If $kB=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $B=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186299860 :question/worked-solution "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the rightmost elements, $6k$ and $2.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [17592186299869 :question/problem "Calculate $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G$, where\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [17592186299869 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $F$ by $4$ and each entry of $G$ by $\\frac{1}{2},$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4F-\\frac{1}{2}G & =4\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 4 & 4\\cdot (-1) & 4\\cdot 0 & 4\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16-1 & -4-0 & 0-2 & 8-(-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -4 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299873 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N$, where\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186299873 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $M$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $N$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}M+2N & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+6 & 0-2 & 10+0 \\\\ -8-4 & 4+1 & 0-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -12 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299897 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299898 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-6)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299899 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299900 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299901 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299902 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299903 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299904 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=5 \\\\ x+3y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299905 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y=7 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299909 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+0y=-8 \\\\ x-3y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [175921862