}"] [17592186301188 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301189 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301195 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301206 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301214 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301219 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186301226 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186302051 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186302055 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302056 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302057 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302061 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302065 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y=8 \\\\ -3x+4y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302066 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302067 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=3 \\\\ 0x+5y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302068 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186302069 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302070 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186302071 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186302080 :question/worked-solution "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}?\\times ? & 2\\times 1 \\\\ 2\\times 3 & 2\\times 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{11}$ must be a $2\\times 3$ block."] [17592186302081 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186302081 :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""] [17592186302082 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186302082 :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186302108 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302109 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & (k+2)y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$"] [17592186302126 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ if $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302126 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the above:\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(-1)-0 \\\\ -6\\cdot 1 \\\\ -4\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186302133 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+1.$"] [17592186302133 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}-3A+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}-3A+I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10-(-6)+1 & 6-18 \\\\ 1-3 & 15-9+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302143 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ (k-2)y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$"] [17592186302193 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302202 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [17592186302202 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"] [17592186302217 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$"] [17592186302217 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$"] [17592186302218 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186302218 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-4+3(-\\frac{2}{3})=-6.$"] [17592186302221 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [17592186302221 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302246 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [17592186302246 :question/worked-solution "We need to reduce the matrix to the row echelon form. For every elementary row operation we also write the equivalent notation in terms of multiplication by the corresponding elementary matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"] [17592186302247 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$"] [17592186302247 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-4.$"] [17592186302265 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.7\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$"] [17592186302265 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186302266 :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$"] [17592186302266 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"] [17592186302267 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302267 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302268 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302268 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302280 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186302280 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the  first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{2}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+3=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302281 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186302281 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{1}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302282 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186302282 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{2}=10\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot 2=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302283 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186302283 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302288 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =19i+7j+k \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302304 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$"] [17592186302304 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=2 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302305 :question/problem "$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}?$"] [17592186302305 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-1$ and ${u}_{33}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=(-1)\\cdot 7=-7.\n$$"] [17592186302306 :question/problem "$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $D=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $D$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}?$"] [17592186302306 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{21}=-2$ and ${u}_{22}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=(-2)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$"] [17592186302308 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$"] [17592186302308 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302309 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302319 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302319 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302324 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$*"] [17592186302324 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(0)} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=-3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302328 :question/problem "Given that\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [17592186302328 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.25){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}B & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ B & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.25,{u}_{33}=-3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-0.75.$"] [17592186302329 :question/problem "Given that\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $K=LU,$ then $L=$"] [17592186302329 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ K & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302331 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302333 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -10 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $B$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$"] [17592186302333 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -10 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,5\\,\\, & 5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,5\\,\\, & 5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{21}=5$ and ${u}_{23}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=5\\cdot (-3)=-15.\n$$"] [17592186302334 :question/problem "$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{22}?$"] [17592186302334 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -7 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -\\frac{1}{2} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${u}_{22}=-6,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{22}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-6)=3.\n$$"] [17592186302335 :question/problem "$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & -6 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $D=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $D$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$"] [17592186302335 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & -6 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & \\,\\frac{1}{3}\\, & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${u}_{23}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9=3.\n$$"] [17592186302361 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t-3 \\\\ 7-9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ -9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-3,7,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302366 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(1,-\\sqrt{2},-3)$ and parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302366 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302371 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 12 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 24 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{34}?$"] [17592186302371 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & -10 & 12 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 24 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 24 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 13 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 24 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 13 & -9 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,3\\,\\, & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain  \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 13 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-2$ and ${u}_{34}=-9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{34}=(-2)\\cdot (-9)=18.\n$$"] [17592186302372 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302373 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ in \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186302373 :question/worked-solution "First, to get the minor of ${d}_{15}$, we remove the $1$st row and $5$th column: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{15}=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cofactor of ${d}_{15}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{15} & =(-1)^{1+5}{M}_{15} \\\\  & ={M}_{15} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302375 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302376 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302377 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302381 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302384 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302385 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=-{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302387 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=-{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302388 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302389 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=-{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302397 :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,2)$ and $Q(2,4,5).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3t \\\\ 3+t \\\\ 2+3t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+3t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302398 :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(3,6,8)$ and $Q(-2,-5,4).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5t \\\\ -11t \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-5t \\\\ 6-11t \\\\ 8-4t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5t \\\\ y=6-11t \\\\ z=8-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302399 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186302399 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=2v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=2.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302400 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}56 \\\\ -98\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186302400 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}56 \\\\ -98\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =14v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=14v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=14.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302401 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186302401 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=4v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=4.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302402 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186302402 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{5}{3}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302403 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186302403 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{3}{4}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186302404 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatri