bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303727 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ \\frac{37}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303731 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303738 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186303749 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303752 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303754 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t}"] [17592186303760 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{2}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{7}^{t/2}"] [17592186303766 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303828 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"] [17592186303830 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303848 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303850 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303897 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303901 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186303912 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{2}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t}"] [17592186303969 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186304053 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186304789 :question/problem "If $g(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u \\\\ 5 \\\\ {u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$, then  $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$"] [17592186304789 :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,5\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u(t)} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u(t)\\end{bmatrix}{u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304796 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304803 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{-5},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(3,1,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨5,1,-5⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304804 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+4t \\\\ 5+5t \\\\ -1+6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,5,-1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186304808 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(1,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186304808 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $n=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304843 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{t}$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$"] [17592186304843 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-3y+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186304844 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+{t}^{2}y=t{e}^{t}$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$"] [17592186304844 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=5{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y+t{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=5{x}_{2}-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+t{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -{t}^{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186304850 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+t\\,{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $3$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$"] [17592186304850 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-t\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-t\\,{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=3{x}_{2}-t\\,{x}_{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -t\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186304912 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186304928 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186304952 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ {{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186304953 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=5{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ {{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186304954 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=-3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186304955 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186304957 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186304958 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186304963 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186304970 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$"] [17592186304970 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186305033 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=\\frac{3}{s+1},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{4}{s-2}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186305033 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{s+1}} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{4}{s-2}} \\\\  & =-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}+4{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1\\cdot t}+4{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}+4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}+4{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305034 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-2}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{2t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186305034 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+{e}^{2t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305035 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=\\frac{1}{2s}+\\frac{7}{2(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=\\frac{3}{2s}-\\frac{7}{2(s-4)}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186305035 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{2s}+\\frac{7}{2(s-4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}+\\frac{7}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{2s}-\\frac{7}{2(s-4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}-\\frac{7}{2}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}.\n$$"] [17592186305036 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{6t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186305036 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ 4-{e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305037 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{4}{s-6},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{4}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{e}^{6t}$\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186305037 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{4}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =1+4{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =1+4{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{4}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =5{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =5-4{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =5-4{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+4{e}^{6t} \\\\ 5-4{e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305052 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186305060 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot 9+{e}^{2t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{2t}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186305061 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot t+(-1) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186305062 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(-5)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186305063 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+0\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186305064 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/89/897778556ebc80f44cf48e17c4fc085c08c45b93358dec9fb0c958de3105c98d.png)"] [17592186305065 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [17592186305065 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [17592186305068 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-3x+y \\\\  & =-3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+4y \\\\  & =2(-1)+4(2) \\\\  & =-2+8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305069 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(-1)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x+3y \\\\  & =-(-1)+3(-2) \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305070 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(3,-1)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-x+4y \\\\  & =-3+4(-1) \\\\  & =-3-4 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+2y \\\\  & =2(3)+2(-1) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305071 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3(0)-2(-2) \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-4x+y \\\\  & =-4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305072 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186305072 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(8) \\\\ 0(12)+3(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+8 \\\\ 0+24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305074 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186305074 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305093 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](images/3f/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)"] [17592186305096 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305099 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305100 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305101 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305102 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305104 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305104 :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{2}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{2}^{-t}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{2}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305105 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305112 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}){e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [17592186305112 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [17592186305119 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)},\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186305121 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186305123 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+2t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [17592186305123 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+2t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial in $t$ of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [17592186305124 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+4 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={X}_{1}(s)+{{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186305125 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)-1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186305126 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/f1/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080d6e.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space. \n\n![](images/a5/a53bb4e6cd7552b92424b168d0cda90fa1085b5e72fbf8a3c37a3a948bdcf9ad.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}}  of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/ecccffb89d43b8de4ce0952d9b8d9686e74670b4d86b29687c76355b70a6ac7d.png)"] [17592186305126 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/f1/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080d6e.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/a5/a53bb4e6cd7552b92424b168d0cda90fa1085b5e72fbf8a3c37a3a948bdcf9ad.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/92/923e79b665f4529e579776188731896e6ce301743394e2102132f2cee7f6eaa9.png)"] [17592186305135 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{7}{10}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186305135 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3b \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3b+4 \\\\ 3a+1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3b+4=-a \\\\ 3a+1=-b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\na=3b-4.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n3(3b-4)+1=-b\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{11}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\na=3\\cdot \\frac{11}{10}-4=\\frac{33}{10}-\\frac{40}{10}=-\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ \\frac{11}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305149 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186305149 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -4\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a \\\\ 5b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a-14 \\\\ 5b-27\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a-14=-4a \\\\ 5b-27=-4b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3a-14=-4a\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5b-27=-4b\\,⇒\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186305170 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186305216 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186305228 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186305233 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186305241 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186305254 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatri