x}"] [17592186308357 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186308357 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{2} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}({a}_{2}-2)t+({b}_{2}+1) \\\\ (-2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8)t+(-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}-2=0 \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8=0 \\\\ {b}_{2}+1={a}_{1} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=0,\\,{b}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308358 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186308358 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3)t+(2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4) \\\\ (3{a}_{2}+2)t+(3{b}_{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ 3{a}_{2}+2=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4={a}_{1} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}-1={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe first two equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9},\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{41}{36}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{41}{36} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308359 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186308359 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1/2 & 3/2 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+\\frac{3}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1)t+(-\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3) \\\\ (\\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2)t+(\\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{7}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{8},\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{11}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308360 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_