atrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"] [17592186308560 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ f(t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308560 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(2+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{2