\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).$"] [17592186308565 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308565 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"] [17592186308581 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308581 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308582 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308582 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308583 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$*"] [17592186308583 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+0\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308584 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308584 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308599 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [17592186308599 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 2t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [17592186308600 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [17592186308600 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [17592186308601 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [17592186308601 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [17592186308609 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186308609 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308611 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308611 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t)={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308613 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$"] [17592186308613 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(4{e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+3\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot {e}^{2t}+1\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{2t}+3\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4{e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4{e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}-0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,b}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."] [17592186308634 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186308634 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(5t)={e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5t{e}^{5t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}(1-5t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{5t}t+{e}^{5t}(1-5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{5t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308636 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,0,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=0.$"] [17592186308636 :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,3,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4+6+18|}{\\sqrt{1+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|28|}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308637 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$"] [17592186308637 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system."] [17592186308644 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ |  | ${e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308644 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}=4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}), \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((3{e}^{4t}-3{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308645 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $0$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2-2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{t}-1$ |  |  |"] [17592186308645 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(0)={e}^{0} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=1 \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the first equation, we immediately get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =-({e}^{t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}+2 \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2 & {e}^{t}-1 \\\\ 2-2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308651 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+1 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/f7/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-1 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/47/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)"] [17592186308652 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)"] [17592186308654 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308654 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 8{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(1+4\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(1-4\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((1+4\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(1+4\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(1+4\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ ({\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308655 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308655 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(-1+2\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(-1-2\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((-1+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(-1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(-1+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308656 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ |  |  |"] [17592186308656 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308657 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308657 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 3\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308658 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308658 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(4\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 4\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308659 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm \\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ |  |  |"] [17592186308659 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308660 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308660 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -13{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{5\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 5\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308661 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"] [17592186308662 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"] [17592186308668 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(4,15,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0.$"] [17592186308668 :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,9,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-5)+(-6)\\cdot 1+7\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10-6-14|}{\\sqrt{25+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-30|}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308670 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3t$ | $1-3t$ |  |  |"] [17592186308670 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308671 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1-3t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308671 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -9{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-9{e}^{t}t \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t & t \\\\ -9t & 1+3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308672 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1-6t$ |  |  |"] [17592186308672 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+6t) \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & =12{e}^{2t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+6t & -3t \\\\ 12t & 1-6t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308673 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1+t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308673 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t{e}^{6t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-t), \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+t & t \\\\ -t & 1-t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308674 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1+4t$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8t$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186308674 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 8{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{3t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(-2t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1-4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+4t & -2t \\\\ 8t & 1-4t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308675 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-5t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1-5t$ |  |  |"] [17592186308675 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5{e}^{t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+5t & -5t \\\\ 5t & 1-5t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308684 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308684 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186308685 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186308685 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186308688 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186308688 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3s}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308692 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186308692 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308695 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$ |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186308695 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308696 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186308696 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308718 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186308718 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308719 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t)-4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |  |"] [17592186308719 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308721 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186308721 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"] [17592186308722 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186308722 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"] [17592186308723 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |"] [17592186308723 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308724 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186308724 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5t}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5t}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatri