2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388432 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388433 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388434 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388435 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388436 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388437 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -6+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4+8\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [17592186388437 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388439 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388440 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388441 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388442 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388443 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388494 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388495 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388496 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388497 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388498 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388500 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388501 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388502 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388503 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388504 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388506 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388507 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388508 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388510 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388511 :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388511 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 8-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{68}\\begin{bmatrix}68 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388513 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388514 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388515 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388516 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388517 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388519 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388520 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388521 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388522 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388523 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388524 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388526 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388527 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388528 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388529 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388530 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388531 :question/problem "For the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [17592186388531 :question/worked-solution "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end