\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [17592186388566 :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388568 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388569 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388570 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388571 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388572 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388625 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388625 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}33 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=33 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5(-3) & =33 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186388627 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388628 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388629 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388630 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388631 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388639 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388641 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388642 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388643 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388644 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388645 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388646 :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388646 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(1-2{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+1=5.$"] [17592186388653 :question/problem "Find ${B}^{120},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[60]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[40]{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388653 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $B:$\n\n$$\nB={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[60]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[40]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $B=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${B}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{B}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{120} & =P{D}^{120}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[60]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[40]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{120}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[60]{2})^{120} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[40]{2})^{120}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -24 \\\\ 12 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 24 \\\\ -48 & -28\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388655 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 24 \\\\ -20 & -44\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388656 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 20 \\\\ -44 & -28\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388657 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 24 \\\\ -48 & -28\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388658 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}48 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -32\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388659 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 36 \\\\ -32 & -24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388660 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B},$ given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186388660 :question/worked-solution "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -20 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388662 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388663 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388664 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -20 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388665 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388666 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -20 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388667 :question/problem "If $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+5\\text{i} & 4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, then $\\,Ax=$"] [17592186388667 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrix by the vector using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3+5\\text{i} & 4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\text{i} & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-3+5\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})+(4-3\\text{i})(4-5\\text{i}) \\\\ (-4\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i})(4-5\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(15+9\\text{i})+(1-32\\text{i}) \\\\ (-12)+(-5-45\\text{i})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16-23\\text{i} \\\\ -17-45\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388669 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}24-20\\text{i} \\\\ 10+51\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388670 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17-45\\text{i} \\\\ 16-23\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388671 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16-23\\text{i} \\\\ -17-45\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388672 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10+51\\text{i} \\\\ 24-20\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388673 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16-27\\text{i} \\\\ 23-35\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388674 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$"] [17592186388674 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=3\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=2\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388676 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388677 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388678 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388679 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388680 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388681 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388681 :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388683 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388684 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388685 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388686 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}29 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388687 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}26 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388688 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388688 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388690 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388691 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388692 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388693 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388694 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388695 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [17592186388695 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388697 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388698 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388699 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388700 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ 24 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388701 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388702 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$"] [17592186388702 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388704 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388705 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388706 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388707 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388708 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388709 :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$"] [17592186388709 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$"] [17592186388716 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388716 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388718 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388719 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388720 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388721 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388722 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388723 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388723 :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388725 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388726 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388727 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388728 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388729 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388730 :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$"] [17592186388730 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388732 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388733 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388734 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388735 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388736 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388737 :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186388737 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-7 \\\\ 4\\text{i}+3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388739 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388740 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388741 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388742 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388743 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388744 :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [17592186388744 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388746 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388747 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388748 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388749 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388750 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388778 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$"] [17592186388778 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388780 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388781 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388782 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388783 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388784 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388785 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186388785 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388787 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388788 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388789 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388790 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388791 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388792 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$"] [17592186388792 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{4}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"] [17592186388799 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388801 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388802 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388803 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388804 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388805 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388806 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [17592186388806 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388808 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388809 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388810 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388811 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388812 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388813 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$"] [17592186388813 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=24,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-24I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-24\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-24I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186388815 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388816 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388817 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388818 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388819 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388820 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"] [17592186388883 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca7c2d5e2fe1729a3b076e63f9fe028ecb459b576f7843ab43e066a19ef89a81.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [17592186388883 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-9,3)$, $(-9,3),$ $(3,-1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/84/84edf48b555f43cfb4866b9798b03110a609c88e92e83ad1fca17a926718a67e.png)"] [17592186388890 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d88ae8667df81624f0a28295c980f846ae88e9b4766fe0d0f692dd75509a56e9.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [17592186388890 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,4)$, $(5,20)$, and  $(2,8).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/94/94247512fd446447144d808c76d00bc9c0d50d4175697597360117eca00b3cf1.png)"] [17592186388897 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [17592186388897 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)"] [17592186388932 :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388934 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388935 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388936 :answer/value "7\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388937 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388938 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388953 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 24 \\\\ -24 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388953 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -24 \\\\ 24 & -7\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-(2) & =-11 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186388955 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388956 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388957 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388958 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388959 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388960 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388960 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388962 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388963 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388964 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388965 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388966 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388967 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388967 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388969 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388970 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388971 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388972 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388973 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388974 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388974 :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388976 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388977 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388978 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388979 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388980 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389002 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389002 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389004 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389005 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389006 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389007 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389008 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389009 :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389009 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14\\cdot 9-9\\cdot 9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -45\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389011 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389012 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389013 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389014 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389015 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389016 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$"] [17592186389016 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-6-(-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389018 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389019 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389020 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389021 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389022 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389035 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75a7519c7953b7a3c5bd5b5e61e961ca26c3f0dc9e1aab32bf2c639dd95325f.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389035 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)"] [17592186389042 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c32886c161e347daa9bf6fa7215f8a09abc4334632c33020c31ade6dfc4e7315.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389042 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/26/266af89426f86d22bc634a231c46a5ad89be14d06bc300a5fa20597998c826fd.png)"] [17592186389049 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bd98626bc49cc3c58e503d5a7373b6af0fcce721cfdf82ff372eaa57d157dda.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389049 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/77/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5ff1c1a7c99b08d04d2ac58d9f8195ee29c8b564873a9abfb2635e93cec1968.png)"] [17592186389181 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [17592186389181 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"] [17592186389188 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?"] [17592186389188 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"] [17592186389195 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [17592186389195 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"] [17592186389202 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?"] [17592186389202 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)"] [17592186389209 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [17592186389209 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"] [17592186389216 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186389250 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v-1 \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."] [17592186389257 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon."] [17592186389381 :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [17592186389381 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})=4x, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})=-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -6y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389383 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} & -{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389384 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ -3y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389385 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6y \\\\ -4x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389386 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -6y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389387 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389388 :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [17592186389388 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389390 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389391 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389392 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389393 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389394 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389395 :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [17592186389395 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2})=2x-y, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2})=-x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y & -x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389397 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x-1 & 2y-1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389398 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y-x \\\\ -y+2x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389399 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y-x & 2x-y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389400 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x-1 \\\\ 2y-1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389401 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y & -x+2y\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389402 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$"] [17592186389402 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389404 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389405 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389406 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389407 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389408 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389421 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}10xy & 5{x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186389421 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}10xy & 5{x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=10xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=5{x}^{2}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy)=10y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}-2y)=10x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy)=10x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389423 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389424 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 20 \\\\ 20 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389425 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389426 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389427 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389567 :question/problem "![](images/61/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$"] [17592186389567 :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n  \n\n![](images/cf/cfe398e1ab7e5b4b487671772bf4b6127e8e141b25491f96c5cc32e77869dec0.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389589 :answer/value "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389590 :answer/value "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389591 :answer/value "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389592 :answer/value "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389593 :answer/value "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389601 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$"] [17592186389601 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389603 :answer/value "x(t)=c\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t},\\,c\\in R"] [17592186389604 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186389605 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186389606 :answer/value "x(t)=c\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,c\\in R"] [17592186389607 :answer/value "x(t)=4\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{c}_{1}t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{{c}_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186389608 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186389608 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2(0)} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n6{c}_{1}=12\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+2{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+1\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389610 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186389611 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186389612 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186389613 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186389614 :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [17592186389615 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=2$ seconds."] [17592186389615 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(0)}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3(0)} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-1+{c}_{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(2)}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{6} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389617 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ 5{e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389618 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-4}+3{e}^{6} \\\\ -{e}^{-4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389619 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4} \\\\ 5{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389620 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{-4}+4{e}^{6} \\\\ {e}^{-4}-4{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389621 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{6} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4}+{e}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389622 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389624 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389625 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389626 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389627 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389628 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389629 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389631 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389632 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389633 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389634 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389635 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389636 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186389638 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389639 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389640 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389641 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389642 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389643 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=5$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{5}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -15 \\\\ -15 & -50\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & -75 \\\\ -30 & -100\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-250,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-250.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-250:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-250I) & =\\begin{bmatrix}200 & -150 \\\\ -150 & -200\\end{bmatrix}+250\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}450 & -150 \\\\ -150 & 50\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-250I))y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389645 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389646 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389647 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389648 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389649 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389855 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n Notice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\n This is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\n the equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\n So, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\n we obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n \n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n As a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n Now, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $||y||=1,$ as usual.\n\n Since we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $12,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=12.\n$$\n\n Next, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=12:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-12I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-12\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Seeking a non-zero solution of $(B-12I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\n Finally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389857 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389858 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389859 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389860 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186389861 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390367 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186390367 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390369 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390370 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390371 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390372 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390373 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390374 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186390374 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390376 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390377 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390378 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390379 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390380 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390381 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390383 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390384 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390385 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390386 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390387 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390388 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186390388 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390390 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390391 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390392 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390393 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390394 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390395 :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390397 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390399 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390400 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390401 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186390402 :question/problem "![](images/fe/fee79ec0b214a4ba87400b4b8c9dab2c623878d067821c572b26ae241625c48a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [17592186390402 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 15 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(2,10)$, $(3,15)$, and  $(5,25).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf5987fde11416780a6a599e3952db7319e1c0e859e2439b7df70c5659bc6839.png)"] [17592186390459 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390460 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390461 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390462 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390463 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390485 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${2}^{A}.$"] [17592186390485 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and the given matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{A}={2}^{(PD{P}^{-1})}=P\\cdot ({2}^{D})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{2}^{D}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{A} & =P\\cdot ({2}^{D})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390487 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390488 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390489 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390490 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390491 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390492 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nG=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$"] [17592186390492 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and the given matrix $G,$ we have\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$ on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+1}$ So, we get\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G & =P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\pi +3 & 4\\pi -3 \\\\ 4\\pi  & -4\\pi \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390494 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi -6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\pi +6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390495 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390496 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pi +6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\pi -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390497 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\pi +3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\pi -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390498 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\pi -3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi +3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390499 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${3}^{K/2}.$"] [17592186390499 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={3}^{x/2}$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{K/2}={3}^{\\frac{1}{2}PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $4$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{3}^{J/2}=\\begin{bmatrix}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{K/2} & =P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-9 \\\\ -18 & 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390501 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390502 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390503 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390504 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390505 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390694 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{b