egin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186394882 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394883 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394884 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394885 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394886 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394887 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$"] [17592186394887 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\