genvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186394958 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394959 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394960 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394961 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394962 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394963 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186394963 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186394965 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394966 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394967 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394968 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394969 :answer/value "\\begin{