}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186394990 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186394998 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186394999 :answer/value "\\be