=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186395005 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395006 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395007 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395008 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395009 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395010 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186395010 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(21)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395012 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395013 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395014 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395015 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395016 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 8\\e