\\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186431859 :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$"] [17592186431860 :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [17592186431860 :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3}$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form   \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"] [17592186431865 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186431868 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186431872 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186431881 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186431884 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186431888 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+2,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+2)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+5(v(t)+1)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-{v}^{2}(t)-2v(t)-1+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+2y(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+2)^{2}-4(u(t)+2)+2(v(t)+1)+2 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4)-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+2+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+4 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431889 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186431890 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186431890 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n